WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:06.299
Now that we've gone over Blueprints, we're next going to take a look really quickly

2
00:00:06.299 --> 00:00:11.119
at Unreal Motion Graphics, or UMG, to understand more about what it is, as we're going to

3
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:16.260
be using this tool with the accompanying workshop.

4
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.059
So what is UMG?

5
00:00:18.059 --> 00:00:23.620
Well UMG, or Unreal Motion Graphics, is a visual UI authoring tool that can be used

6
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:29.260
to create UI elements such as in-game HUDs, menus, or other interface-related graphics

7
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.739
you wish to present to your users.

8
00:00:31.739 --> 00:00:38.259
You can use UMG to create menus, full screens, or pop-ups, HUDs, and even 3D displays.

9
00:00:38.259 --> 00:00:45.670
Right here is an image of the UMG editor, and the editor itself is actually broken

10
00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.729
up into a couple of different tools.

11
00:00:47.729 --> 00:00:48.729
There's two different tools.

12
00:00:48.729 --> 00:00:51.009
There's the designer, which we see right here.

13
00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:54.329
The designer is broken up into the following little areas here.

14
00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:55.729
One, we have the palette.

15
00:00:55.729 --> 00:01:01.530
This is where we're going to find all of the pre-built UI elements that we want to

16
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:02.530
work with.

17
00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:06.250
So the great thing about this is that we can pull in something like a generic button

18
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:12.760
and either use the existing imagery or bring in all of our own imagery.

19
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:17.280
This is really handy because we don't actually have to worry about building code to make

20
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.280
a button.

21
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.579
We simply just bring in different common UI elements like buttons and images and sliders

22
00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:24.719
and things like that.

23
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:30.030
And then we can use those to do things in our UI.

24
00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:34.409
Underneath our palette here, we have our hierarchy, which is going to show us the hierarchy

25
00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:37.109
of this UI.

26
00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:40.349
So if we have a bunch of buttons here, it will show us the buttons, and if we have

27
00:01:40.349 --> 00:01:45.390
things nested underneath those buttons, it will show us what those items are.

28
00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:49.890
Three in the middle, this is going to be our UI preview area.

29
00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.670
What we do with our UMG widgets is we bring these common elements into three right

30
00:01:54.670 --> 00:02:03.549
here and then we can place them around the scene in order to make them more in line

31
00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:05.959
with our UI needs.

32
00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:09.759
Finally, or I shouldn't say finally, but next we have four, which is our details

33
00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:10.759
panel.

34
00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:15.319
The details panel is context sensitive, so that means that based on what we have selected,

35
00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:19.919
the details inside of here are going to be different, but this is how we modify the

36
00:02:19.919 --> 00:02:27.080
properties of these common elements that we put inside of our UI right here.

37
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:31.199
Last but not least is number five, which is our animations.

38
00:02:31.199 --> 00:02:35.599
This allows us to animate objects, so if I was to click on this, we'd get a little

39
00:02:35.599 --> 00:02:40.120
animation window coming up, and while we're not going to be utilizing this in today's

40
00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:46.280
tutorial, it is nice to know that we can fully animate all of our UI elements, their

41
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:53.479
positions, but we can also animate their visibility or we can make them fly up and

42
00:02:53.479 --> 00:02:56.759
then get bigger or smaller, lots of cool things that we can do with that.

43
00:02:56.759 --> 00:03:03.280
Now the second part of our tool is called the graph, so I like to refer to them like

44
00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.280
this.

45
00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:09.280
The designer is how we make our UI pretty, the graph is how we make our UI functional.

46
00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.520
If you notice that this actually looks very similar to a blueprint, and that's because

47
00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.000
you are actually using blueprints to build your UI.

48
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.840
So over here, under the My Blueprint, this is where we're going to find different functions

49
00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:25.400
that we have built, or right now we're seeing pre-existing functions in there, or

50
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.120
we can also override and add variables and things like that.

51
00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.199
Two is our details panel, and again, this is context sensitive, so based on what

52
00:03:33.199 --> 00:03:37.800
we have selected, the details is going to be different, and then three is our event

53
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.840
graph, and this is going to work exactly the same like the event graph does inside

54
00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:42.840
of blueprints.

55
00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.520
So we're going to put down various nodes inside of here, and then based off of the

56
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.879
events that happen when those nodes are clicked on, we will then do things.

57
00:03:51.879 --> 00:03:55.479
So for example, if we were to be inside of our, I'm going to click back to our

58
00:03:55.479 --> 00:03:59.639
designer here, inside of our designer, we could put something like a button here,

59
00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:02.719
and then on that button, we're going to have a little behavior that says On Clicked,

60
00:04:02.719 --> 00:04:07.560
and when we press that, we'll see that inside of our event graph here, we'll have

61
00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:11.319
a little On Clicked that will allow us to then execute some code.

62
00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:15.879
So this might look overwhelming or a little daunting, do not worry, you're going to

63
00:04:15.879 --> 00:04:21.759
see how easy these tools are to use and how much blueprints and UMG all have

64
00:04:21.759 --> 00:04:25.360
together in our workshop, which we're going to start right after this slide.
